# JPEG frame_header all_default lever: HONEST-STOP (2026-05-28)

## Verdict: STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE — no source change ships.

The lever (LEVERS_ROUND2 #1) hypothesised that JPEG frames could short-circuit
the frame header to a 1-bit `all_default` flag, saving ~10-20 bits per file
(~200-400 bytes across a 200-file bench at +1.184% above cjxl-e7).

After tracing libjxl's `AllDefault` visitor (`fields.cc:116-153`), the
frame_header field visitor (`frame_header.cc:215-220`), the JPEG frame setup
(`enc_frame.cc:349-507`), and the decoder-side smoothing gate
(`dec_frame.cc:344-358`), the lever is **structurally impossible** for ANY
JPEG frame that libjxl itself can produce.

## Why every JPEG frame is non-all-default

`AllDefaultVisitor::Bits / U32 / U64 / Bool` (`fields.cc:120-141`) compares
the current value of each visited field against the `default_value` literal
passed at the visit site. For a JPEG frame, libjxl forces at least three
fields off the default:

1. **`flags = 0x80` (kSkipAdaptiveDCSmoothing)**, default `0`
   - `enc_frame.cc:501-503`: `if (jpeg_data) { frame_header->UpdateFlag(true,
     FrameHeader::kSkipAdaptiveDCSmoothing); }`
   - Required for decode correctness: `dec_frame.cc:344-358` runs
     `AdaptiveDCSmoothing` on `dc_storage` in-place when the flag is unset.
     For JPEG reconstruction, DC coefficients are exact integers from the
     source JPEG bitstream; smoothing them would mutate the reconstructed
     JPEG bytes (roundtrip break). So `flags = 0` is not an option even as
     an experimental override.
   - libjxl's U64Coder costs 2+8=10 bits for `0x80` (selector 2: 17..272).

2. **`color_transform = kYCbCr` for YCbCr / grayscale JPEGs** ⇒ visited
   `alternate = true`, default `false` (`frame_header.cc:244-249`).
   - `enc_frame.cc:376-377` ⇒ `SetColorTransformFromJpegData`
     (`enc_jpeg_data.cc:240-283`) returns `kYCbCr` for 1-component (always)
     and for 3-component without an Adobe APP14 transform=0 marker / R,G,B
     component IDs. That covers ~99% of real JPEGs (YCbCr is the JFIF
     standard).
   - Only 3-component Adobe-RGB or explicit-RGB-ID JPEGs land in `kNone`
     and survive THIS field, but they still fail (1) above.
   - Costs 1 bit for the `alternate` flag.

3. **`loop_filter.gab = false`, `epf_iters = 0`** for JPEG (defaults
   `true`, `2` per `loop_filter.cc:27, 58`).
   - `LoopFilterFromParams` (`enc_frame.cc:302-345`) sets `gab = false`
     because `cparams.gaborish` is `kDefault` and
     `cparams.butteraugli_distance == 0` ⇒ predicate evaluates false.
     `epf_iters` defaults to 0 in the lossless branch (incl. JPEG, since
     `cparams.IsLossless()` is true for transcoding).
   - This makes `loop_filter` ITSELF non-all-default at the nested visitor
     (`loop_filter.cc:21-25`). But the loop_filter section is still written
     via its own nested all_default flag — irrelevant to the OUTER
     frame_header all_default because the OUTER all_default is already
     forced off by (1) and (2).

## Why setting flags=0 anyway would break roundtrip

For YCbCr 4:4:4 JPEGs, `dec_frame.cc:206-212` does NOT enforce
kSkipAdaptiveDCSmoothing (only non-444 chroma requires it). So a 4:4:4
JPEG with `flags=0` would DECODE without error — but `dec_frame.cc:349-353`
would then run `AdaptiveDCSmoothing(quantizer.MulDC(), &dc_storage, ...)`,
mutating the DC plane in place. AC decode and JPEG-coefficient
reconstruction read from the mutated `dc_storage`, so the reconstructed
JPEG bytes would diverge from the source. That's a roundtrip break.

No code path is benefiting from this lever, in either libjxl or our
encoder. The current `is_all_default()` gate at `xyb_encoded == true`
(jxl-encoder `src/headers/frame_header.rs:712`) is correct as written.

## What we currently write (YCbCr 4:4:4 JPEG, no animation, no crop)

(Counted from `src/headers/frame_header.rs:328-487` and `:640-669`.)

| field                | bits | notes                                            |
| ---                  | ---: | ---                                              |
| all_default          |    1 | =0                                               |
| frame_type           |    2 | =Regular (0)                                     |
| encoding             |    1 | =VarDct (0)                                      |
| flags (U64 0x80)     |   10 | U64Coder selector 2: 2 selector + 8 payload      |
| do_ycbcr             |    1 | =true                                            |
| jpeg_upsampling (3x) |    6 | 3 × 2 bits, all zero for 4:4:4                   |
| upsampling (U32 1)   |    2 | selector 0                                       |
| num_passes (U32 1)   |    2 | selector 0                                       |
| have_crop            |    1 | =false                                           |
| blend mode           |    2 | =Replace (selector 0); source NOT written        |
| is_last              |    1 | =true                                            |
| name length          |    2 | selector 0 (length 0)                            |
| lf_all_default       |    1 | =false                                           |
| gab                  |    1 | =false                                           |
| epf_iters            |    2 | =0                                               |
| lf extensions (U64)  |    2 | =0                                               |
| frame extensions     |    2 | =0                                               |
| **total**            | **39** |                                                |

If the lever were possible, savings would be 38 bits (~5 bytes) per frame.
At 200 files × 5 bytes = ~1000 bytes ≈ -0.0001% on a 5MB bench. The task's
"~10-20 bits" estimate was optimistic on the per-file delta and the lever
is unreachable anyway. Total potential savings if the lever WERE possible:
sub-percent of the residual +1.184% gap.

